{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action","id":629016,"title":"Twilight Judge Merit in Action?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-08-16T13:27:11-07:00","id":629016,"title":"Twilight Judge Merit in Action?","content":"Hi,<br /> <br /> Anyone got experience using the Twilight Judge merit in a game?<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_J83T-t9g-87pvtUTKCZIJ2QQzCTOzUclkWJjsvb814/edit\" target=\"_blank\"><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1_J83T-t9g-87pvtUTKCZIJ2QQzCTOzUclkWJjsvb814/edit\" target=\"_blank\">https://docs.google.com/document/d/1...WJjsvb814/edit</a></a><br /> <br /> What recourse would a character have if an NPC with a vindictive grudge were to wield this against them? At what rate could their Ordo Status be removed? How could someone with the Merit be opposed or removed from their position?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/291-scutarii","name":"Scutarii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"702"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T03:54:33-07:00","id":629788,"title":"","content":"It depends on what materials you're using. In the Ordo Dracul book, it was not unheard of for a Dying Light member to use their authority once they obtained it to strike back at those who gave them shit or hindered them in the first place. (Be careful who you piss on climbing the status ladder because it could come around and bite you in the ass.) The entire purpose of that oath is to arbitrate disputes that members couldn't handle internally and ensure the Ordo Dracul collectively stayed focused on their transcendence research. They were the final word, so you have no recourse once a decision is rendered by a Twilight Judge. Even in the current description, they're supposed to be considered individuals who have already earned not only the respect, but a measure of trust from all the other Ordo Dracul mysteries. You don't get someone entrenched like that removed easily short of murdering them outright.<br /> <br /> Are you asking about their removal from standpoint of the the scope of mechanics or from a roleplaying perspective?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T04:51:44-07:00","id":629806,"title":"","content":"Two judges antagonists to each other in the same city might be a fun dynamic <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3648-nepher","name":"Nepher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3648_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T05:18:03-07:00","id":629817,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>tsusasi</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action?p=629788#post629788\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It depends on what materials you're using. In the Ordo Dracul book, it was not unheard of for a Dying Light member to use their authority once they obtained it to strike back at those who gave them shit or hindered them in the first place. (Be careful who you piss on climbing the status ladder because it could come around and bite you in the ass.) The entire purpose of that oath is to arbitrate disputes that members couldn't handle internally and ensure the Ordo Dracul collectively stayed focused on their transcendence research. They were the final word, so you have no recourse once a decision is rendered by a Twilight Judge. Even in the current description, they're supposed to be considered individuals who have already earned not only the respect, but a measure of trust from all the other Ordo Dracul mysteries. You don't get someone entrenched like that removed easily short of murdering them outright.<br /> <br /> Are you asking about their removal from standpoint of the the scope of mechanics or from a roleplaying perspective?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It's more that the line in the merit talking about nepostism and conflict of interest not being an issue, if that's not considered an issue then how does an individual garner that level of widespread respect or keep it? That sounds like an environment in which they'd be encouraged to abuse their position which is the kind of thing that would undermine that broad respect. Censuring the wrong person could undermine a whole load of support. What happens if they lose that broad base of support?<br /> <br /> Unfortunately murder is not an option as that leads to a Blood Hunt on the killer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/291-scutarii","name":"Scutarii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"702"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T07:02:25-07:00","id":629882,"title":"","content":"I would say that once reached, this kind of power is precisely that powerful. It was earned by trust, but now the Judge is free to abuse power, what is very in-character for a vampiric institution, even with a necessity for trust.<br /> <br /> That said, this kind of character usually runs for the Extreme Measure Pitfall: she will keep her cool, because not doing so calls for extreme non-official responses. As far as you keep powerful allies, you can abuse authority with impunity, but as soon as you become a liability for lack of decorum, you can count on your authority being ignored and eventually your enemies finding you, with no one interested in watching for you.<br /> <br /> A Blood Hunt, if even happening, can still be a very moot process, only meant to show that the law still technically exists. You know, Destruction isn't a Tradition anymore...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T11:30:37-07:00","id":630096,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Scutarii</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action?p=629817#post629817\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's more that the line in the merit talking about nepostism and conflict of interest not being an issue, if that's not considered an issue then how does an individual garner that level of widespread respect or keep it? That sounds like an environment in which they'd be encouraged to abuse their position which is the kind of thing that would undermine that broad respect. Censuring the wrong person could undermine a whole load of support. What happens if they lose that broad base of support?</div> </div> </div> </div>When you think of a Twilight Judge you should think of Stephen Hawking not Antonin Scalia. A Twilight Judge is, first and foremost, a scientist. They are drawn from the ranks of the Sworn of the Dying Light, whose task is to continue research into the coils of the dragon so as to find a way to transcend the curse of vampirism. The credibility of a Twilight Judge rests on their mastery of the coils and their creation of new coils and new scales. Even when they are personally disliked they can still command the respect of their peers because of their accomplishments in the lab.<br /> <br /> For example:<br /> <br /> <i><b>No one in the Ordo actually likes the Nosferatu called &quot;Black Gregory&quot;; he's creepy and he smells bad. However, they respect him because he completely mastered the Mystery of the Ascendant and developed &quot;scales&quot; in the form of alchemical stimulants which can allow any vampire to maintain the blush of life all night long. The trade in these stimulants has won the Ordo great influence with the other covenants and brought in a lot of resources. Between those accomplishments, and his well honed abilities to attune wyrm's nests, his election to the office of Twilight Judge was assured. </b></i><br /> <br /> One could argue that a Twilight Judge is trusted with the power they are given because their scientific successes prove they are intelligent and objective. However, it could also be that the Ordo cares more about results than it does personality when doling out political influence. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6617-vyrkolash","name":"Vyrkolash","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-17T23:41:10-07:00","id":630762,"title":"","content":"So Twilight Judge goes to the people doing the research? I thought it would have gone to the political wing of the Ordo.<br /> <br /> Where are you getting this information on how it operates in setting by the way?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/291-scutarii","name":"Scutarii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"702"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T03:47:46-07:00","id":630919,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Scutarii</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action?p=630762#post630762\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Twilight Judge goes to the people doing the research? I thought it would have gone to the political wing of the Ordo.<br /> <br /> Where are you getting this information on how it operates in setting by the way?</div> </div> </div> </div>In first edition, all of the sworn oaths were political. Each one is assigned an area of concern where their particular order has primacy in regards to everyone else. The Axe was in charge of covenant security and when/if the covenant is at war, The Sworn of Mysteries were in charge when matters entailed some kind of formal policy of involvement with non covenant members or representing the covenant to the city at large. The Sworn of the Dying Light were in charge of directing covenant research, formal sentencing for violations of the covenant's rules, adjudicating disputes between members, and deciding which branch has primacy in situations where authority is unclear or it overlaps with multiple groups. (Nepotism and conflicts of interest weren't an issue then either.) The Twilight Judge was a seniormost member of the Dying Light determined by how many coils they knew.<br /> <br /> In the scenario Vyrkolash outlined, Black Gregory may have created a new scale and offered to sell them to individuals outside the covenant but 'technically' he would not be allowed to do so without approval from a Mysteries Sworn. Obviously this would entail more deal brokering. Likely in the form of ruling in favor of something the Mysteries Sworn would want or maybe authorizing them to study a subject formerly considered taboo.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5466,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","user":"tsusasi","date":". 08-18-2015, 06:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5466-tsusasi","name":"tsusasi","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5466_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T03:52:16-07:00","id":630928,"title":"","content":"The Ordo Dracul book has the info. The reason it isn't in the &quot;political wing&quot; is because those Sworn are dedicated to directed the Covenant as a whole and dealing with outsiders. The researchers are the ones that deal with <i>internal</i> matters affecting an <i>individual</i>. The researchers direct individuals in the covenant as it is sometimes required during their research. The Sworn of Mysteries are actually more like legislators and are prohibited from making judgments for single members, so the Dying Light are more like the research/judicial branch. Dying Light interprets the rules handed down by the Sworn of Mysteries. Technically, the Sworn of the Mysteries should be able to &quot;overrule&quot; a Twilight Judge in some cases, but it wouldn't be retroactive and so it would fall more under ST fiat. If a Twilight Judge starts making judgments that seem out of character for the Ordo Dracul then you could assume the Merit is lost.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T04:13:13-07:00","id":630995,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Scutarii</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action?p=630762#post630762\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So Twilight Judge goes to the people doing the research? I thought it would have gone to the political wing of the Ordo.<br /> <br /> Where are you getting this information on how it operates in setting by the way?</div> </div> </div> </div> Sworn of the Axe were obviously the warriors, but they also got first pick of any spoils, even if it was the spoils of investigating a haunted house where they did nothing. They are in charge during times of war. Sworn of the Dying Light are not only the researchers, but also the judges of the Dragons, and have the ability to settle any disputes. Sworn of the Mysteries are the socialites and politicians, and they get to decide the course of the overall Order when not in war or research.<br /> <br /> In short, each of the Sworn is either Mental, Physical, or Social focused. Being a judge is actually a -mental- aspect, since it requires study of law and precidents, and not social. It seems like a social thing in the US, because we elect our judges, but many other countries appoint their judges based on qualifications, and not social ability.<br /> <br /> This information comes from the <i>Rites of the Dragon</i> and <i>Ordo Dracul</i> books, who've covered the Sworn in more depth than we've seen in the core. <br /> <br /> <br /> As for its <i>use</i>? I actually haven't seen it come up in play, but that's because I haven't had a game that's been Ordo focused yet. Ordo politics haven't really come up in such depth where the PCs have considered it. Some games make use of such merits more than others. Now, if we were in a heavy Ordo Dracul game? I could see a lot of potential. Right now, not so much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 08-18-2015, 07:17 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T04:18:37-07:00","id":630999,"title":"","content":"I thought it would be a political position because it sounded like it needed broad consensus and support from multiple parties, which is the kind of thing a political/social member would be good at building up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/291-scutarii","name":"Scutarii","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar291_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"702"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T13:22:13-07:00","id":631461,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems like a social thing in the US, because we elect our judges,</div> </div> </div> </div>This line actually surprised me more than any other in he thread...<br /> <br /> Broad consensus is, in truth, antagonistic for support for a political Kindred. Being highly social and political means to make enemies and take sides. In a society like the Kindred (and actually on human societies this also happens a lot), such a position requires compromise from all the parts. So it's not about everyone being happy, but about no-one being too unhappy. And academic researchers are also a common choice in this regard.<br /> <br /> Of course, it doesn't means such a position precludes some politicking, and you'll probably find the best politics among the researchers taking it. If you ever saw how things work in the Academy, there is an extremely political place anyway.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T14:45:44-07:00","id":631569,"title":"","content":"Well, this is Vampire. EVERYTHING is socio-political. Perhaps I should have chosen a better word than social... I mean that, while the United States elect judges into their positions, in Europe judges (generally speaking) aren't elected but appointed, because you don't want them to try and cater to popular opinion. The Ordo Dracul is unsurprisingly based on the academic achievement over the non-election model - that's the common style in the region the Ordo is from! All in all, judges being more academics than politicans fits the Old World feel of the covenant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T15:19:34-07:00","id":631633,"title":"","content":"Academia and scientific research communities are extremely political. The people running research have very little experience with the research they oversee. If you state an unpopular opinion as a university professor, your career is over at that university. So for the real models that Dying Light would be based on rising in prestige and authority is all about how well you lie and kiss ass. It makes sense for one of the highest positions to be a political position that decides without regard to impartiality or conflicts of interest. That is exactly how the scientific community works. A member believes what they want despite little evidence or evidence to the contrary and as long as they convince enough of the others that they are correct then it is considered fact. Want a scientific study that asbestos exposure has no link to the development of cancer? Write a check. With enough zeros on that check you can get the whole world to believe it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T16:18:36-07:00","id":631701,"title":"","content":"Not so drastic IRL, but more or less on those lines.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action?p=631569#post631569\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, this is Vampire. EVERYTHING is socio-political. Perhaps I should have chosen a better word than social... I mean that, while the United States elect judges into their positions, in Europe judges (generally speaking) aren't elected but appointed, because you don't want them to try and cater to popular opinion. The Ordo Dracul is unsurprisingly based on the academic achievement over the non-election model - that's the common style in the region the Ordo is from! All in all, judges being more academics than politicans fits the Old World feel of the covenant.</div> </div> </div> </div>I understood, I just didn't knew that judges where elected in the USA.<br /> <br /> By the way. There are other places outside USA than Europe. I'm from Brazil, and judges here are selected through 8-stages exams. It is, indeed, an academic feat. Once in the position, a judge can still be evicted by several (if hard) means, usually including a decision by a council of peers.<br /> <br /> Sorry, but though our system have its problems, I have a hard time thinking of a judge not being selected by virtue of his grasp of the science of Law. What are the requisites and electoral process?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-18T18:53:01-07:00","id":631820,"title":"","content":"I was speaking of the US. I can only hope things are better in other places in the world. The science isn't any more reliable, but I have no idea why. The main issue with research is the conclusions rather than methodology, which tends to be much more sound.<br /> <br /> Higher judges are appointed, sometimes with approval by a legislative body, but end up political hacks about half of the time. Since there isn't a huge overlap between politics and the law, it isn't frequently a problem.<br /> <br /> Lower judges are elected, occasionally appointed and then re-elected, usually without any debate or campaigning. They can serve as long as they are re-elected which is easy as it usually is a vote to confirm.<br /> <br /> Judges have to pass a test required by all lawyers to practice law in the state. I'm not aware of other requirements. They must be minor if any. Higher judges are chosen from lower judges.<br /> <br /> Despite all that some are quite knowledgeable about the law. Go figure.<br /> <br /> *Judges always run without a political party affiliation and are expected to keep it secret, maintaining an appearance of being apolitical.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1526,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","user":"Diggs","date":". 08-18-2015, 10:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-19T00:15:31-07:00","id":632035,"title":"","content":"Well, as far as I could see at university, science is a political game all around the world. It isn't nearly as devoid of reliability as some people want to say, but isn't without its vices, either. The best way to deal with it is to internalize the scientific method to learn how to analyze data by yourself, but that isn't simple. Most scientists never actually do that, and it isn't their fault.<br /> <br /> About judges, I do find the legal system of the USA weird as a general rule, and now I understand the difficulty to get the Ordo Dracul system. Here in Brazil not only the system to select Lower Judges is different (for Higher, not so much, but they will come from this body of hard-tested Judges), we barely use the jury system. Our legal system is almost completely based on the notion of law as a science, and decisions being made by an impartial specialist, hence the extra exams to be a Judge (our lawyers also need to pass a similar test, it isn't an easy one, and you have to be a lawyer first to apply for the exam to be a Judge).<br /> <br /> It isn't devoid of problems. We actually have few ways to make a &quot;quality-control&quot; of our Judges, so sometimes they get to be problematic. But most of the time <u>this</u> aspect of the system seems to work. With such a hard time to get to be a Judge in the first place, all the benefits and stability of the position, and harsh consequences for being caught doing shit with their work, the Brazilian Judges have few reasons to fool around on work.<br /> <br /> So I kind of get the Twilight Judge thing. For me it makes all sense that they put confidence in a scholar with (seemingly) few political ties and a lot of knowledge about the maters at hand, give them a lot of benefits, power and stability, knowing that if this person manages to royally screw things up, the trip to Hell will be quick and complete.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-19T04:06:07-07:00","id":632141,"title":"","content":"In the USA, any good scientist knows to disregard the conclusions of others (foreign or domestic), consider the data analysis suspect, and test the methodology. Basically, look at what someone else did, try to repeat, and figure out what it means yourself. It makes the process of research needlessly lengthy, but it is what you have to do.<br /> <br /> We have a saying that statistics can show anything a statistician wants them to show. Two people can take the exact same data and &quot;prove&quot; opposing conclusions. It doesn't mean science is wrong often, just untrustworthy unless tested hundreds of times by different people. This is actually in keeping with traditional scientific methods of remaining skeptical until you observe evidence supporting a conclusion. Failure of the system results from not enough skepticism. DTA - &quot;Don't trust anybody&quot;<br /> <br /> I think we use a jury to determine guilt because our system of providing judges is suspect. I think any other method to create judges would be corrupted, so I'm not sure that there is a better way available here.<br /> <br /> As I said to a German friend, what works there won't work here and vice versa. In the USA, the legal system is based on an old British legal system and deeply rooted in the foundations of the government. It isn't practical to change it drastically, perhaps, unfortunately.<br /> <br /> I can see why Twilight Judges exist how they do. I expect it would work out. If the TJ is bad they get replaced. If they are smart, they step down.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-21T09:59:50-07:00","id":634736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action?p=631701#post631701\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">By the way. There are other places outside USA than Europe. I'm from Brazil, and judges here are selected through 8-stages exams. It is, indeed, an academic feat. Once in the position, a judge can still be evicted by several (if hard) means, usually including a decision by a council of peers.</div> </div> </div> </div> I don't mean to be exclusive - its just that US and European law are the only two I'm familiar with personally, given that US law was an attempt to &quot;correct&quot; the flaws of the basic european system (and failed miserably, making things worse, in my opinion). <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Sorry, but though our system have its problems, I have a hard time thinking of a judge not being selected by virtue of his grasp of the science of Law. What are the requisites and electoral process? </div> </div> </div> In the US, it depends on the district in question. Each state and territory have the power to write own rules, as does the federal government. There are few country-wide rules on the matter.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Diggs</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/629016-twilight-judge-merit-in-action?p=631820#post631820\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was speaking of the US. I can only hope things are better in other places in the world.</div> </div> </div> </div> From what I understand, the US model is one of the more inefficient ones, but each has its own problems. The entire system of law enforcement within the US needs revision, to be honest - its broken in so many ways and can be made better, but the legislaters resist change for their own political reasons. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> We have a saying that statistics can show anything a statistician wants them to show. Two people can take the exact same data and &quot;prove&quot; opposing conclusions. It doesn't mean science is wrong often, just untrustworthy unless tested hundreds of times by different people. This is actually in keeping with traditional scientific methods of remaining skeptical until you observe evidence supporting a conclusion. Failure of the system results from not enough skepticism. DTA - &quot;Don't trust anybody&quot; </div> </div> </div> The problem isn't even that the science is untrustworthy. Its that the people publishing the material lie on it. Its the human component that's wrong. <br /> <br /> For example, drug trials. The scientific community discovered that, with all the massive studies medical companies are doing on making new drugs? They're only releasing half the data they collect. Unsurprisingly, its the data that's supporting the conclusion they want, while throwing out what they don't want.<br /> <br /> In another example, people are misled by improper sample sizes and deliberately misleading words on the statistics. We actually a case in Congress about someone quoting &quot;Cumulitive Percentages&quot; as a case for why drug use was going down. These huge numbers the spokesmen quoted, with15 and 20? They meant nothing - they just added a bunch of other percentages together to get a number and made it look impressive. <br /> <br /> A third example comes from crime statistics. They're a joke - it not only doesn't accurately reflect crime in a neighborhood, but actual crimes -reported- to the police or found by cops. There are no standards on how to collect the data, there is evidence of fudging the numbers so that murders and thefts were renamed as suicides, or just unreported. You could have a dangerous neighborhood look awesome by the numbers, simply because the poeple and the cop aren't giving correct data to be analyzed. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T13:15:58-07:00","id":636215,"title":"","content":"The DTA approach is quite standard around the world, because it is impossible to actually get rid of the &quot;human element&quot;. Simply put, with or without political agendas (and most of the time you can count a &quot;with&quot;), it is impossible to be completely impartial about any mater, or to be sure of the precision of any method. You can make quite a mess with your conclusion about some data without any intention or missing data whatsoever. And, of course, there's a lot of room for intention and missing data.<br /> <br /> But in science proper this kind of corrects itself with time and a large dose of DTA approach. The bigger problem nowadays is how to make this correction translate from the scientific community to the general public and the practical uses of science. Decisions, products, opinions and policies are produced out of &quot;bad&quot; science everyday, everywhere. There are even methods to correct to a degree (and, more importantly, have a grasp of the degree of error of) the data produced out of unreliable sources, like crime statistics. Its a shame such methods will rarely make it to the lay public, or be used to actually do anything.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I personally can't think that a lay jury should have the power to decide over the destiny of someone. It is to ask for emotions and double-standards to keep in the way, instead of the law and the actual evidences. Sorry, but the idea that people are being imprisoned or set free by such a system terrifies me. The public opinion is already a dangerous and unfair weapon without such power. Even in Brazil I'm against the few stances where we use the jury system (with a lot more of oversee by the judge).<br /> <br /> I'm curious particularly by the Japanese system, where both lawyers are actually also the chief detectives. It is their job to conduce investigation and draw proper conclusions. Then the judge ask for details and conduce the trial, by what I understood, being the one to ask questions to witnesses or the lawyers. Currently the main problem with the Japanese system is that the difficulties of starting a case makes a lot of prosecutors to never start one unless the guilt of the accused is under no doubt.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-22T18:51:16-07:00","id":636531,"title":"","content":"The jury is to give advantage to the accused. The US judicial system is heavily biased towards defendants in criminal trials. Elements of a crime are easily explained so convincing 12 people a person actually committed a crime without a reasonable doubt with a judge to oversee the affair to ensure proper procedures are followed is effective when a person is definitely guilty except when the judge is incompetent, lawyers are corrupt, and the defendant is famous which influences the jury on an emotional level. Without the problems, the innocent would almost never be found guilty. The system was always intended to favor the person on trial so the guilty would sometimes go free. At times when the jury has been excluded, historically, we've had serious problems with the innocent being punished for crimes they didn't commit. With a jury that happens due to corruption and incompetence of the people with the law degrees. As I said before, significant changes would require a whole new system from the ground up to work.<br /> <br /> I think it is universal everywhere that the rich and famous usually get treated better than they should while the poor get screwed. I don't think it is different in Japan where the organized crime force people to confess to crimes they didn't commit and the investigators often will only decide a death was murder if they believe they can find out who the perpetrator was instead ruling deaths as suicide or accident. The &quot;success&quot; of the Japanese legal system is not due to great detective work and a superior court system. The difficulty is not in starting a case, but instead not wanting the shame of a negative mark on their record by failing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T00:18:40-07:00","id":637754,"title":"","content":"Convincing 12 lay people of something is far different from asserting truth. And I heavily doubt that a lay jury is more efficient at not punishing the innocent. It is far more likely to work on cultural bias instead of active corruption, but corruption is something you can fight against.<br /> <br /> About Japan, the little a could research, they already did some serious analysis about the system there. Of course, DTA, but it seems consistent that the system is largely outweighed, and the state prosecutors, the ones responsible for deciding what case will be brought to the judge, tend to only bring cases with almost certainty of guilt. Most crimes are never judged, and the police do a reprimand at their own discretion. And then, I don't know much about Japanese police.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T02:16:41-07:00","id":637788,"title":"","content":"A jury can't have a cultural bias when they are the same culture as the accused. The US did away with all white juries deciding the fate of a black defendant, for instance. The defense attorney actually gets to weigh in on picking the jury to make sure nobody on the jury is biased against their client. They might fail if they are incompetent, of course. The corruption resulting in the innocent being punished is a result of prosecutors that are interested in convictions rather than finding out the truth. Many prosecutors are okay with convicting a person that <i>probably</i> committed the crime. As the corruption is moral rather than legal there isn't much that can be done about it. We try, leading to the inefficiency problem. In a perfect situation with the judge and lawyers doing what they are supposed to do, the jury would work to avoid wrong convictions, but we don't live in a perfect world. Because the world isn't perfect, the US legal system ends up being as flawed as any other. Of course, it is impossible to really determine which country convicts the most people wrongly or lets the guilty free.<br /> <br /> I'm not a lawyer so I have no idea how a legal system can be fixed. I can only speak as to how the US system would work in an ideal case and how it fails because reality is not ideal. I've seen other places that don't use a jury and the same thing happens, innocent are punished and guilty set free. In the US the innocent are found guilty when they are poor. You won't find any rich, innocent people in prison. The people in between would be found innocent, but likely end up poor in the process. This is as fair an analysis of what happens as I know to make about the US legal system. I know some places have it worse. The rest that I have any familiarity with end up in the same place, the poor are screwed.<br /> <br /> In the US poor communities complain about an unjust legal system. They blame cultural biases, but that isn't right. Prosecutors learn to game the juries they deal with and since the defendants are poor they don't get a defense equal to what they face. When those same prosecutors move out to other communities and face excellent attorneys, they can't get a conviction because they aren't used to facing a real challenge. The poor get railroaded, but it isn't because they face a jury instead of a judge. They can choose to face a judge instead of a jury. The poor would still get railroaded in the US.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T03:02:35-07:00","id":637813,"title":"","content":"The poor get railroaded almost everywhere, but the problem is more complex than that. So I will stick to my point about lay jury.<br /> <br /> I wasn't clear (partially because I was texting from a cellphone, so I couldn't use my usual huge word-count...) about what I meant with cultural bias. It wasn't about judging based on prejudice for another culture. By definition, cultural bias is &quot;interpreting phenomena based o your own cultural expectations&quot;. One famous american case, and just an example with probably more occurrences, was about two teenagers who where viewed as strange and potentially problematic by the community. They where from the same culture, but where also a deviation of said culture (a normal thing to occur at any culture). When accused of a killing, the prosecutor just took evidence of how they where odd and dangerous, without any real proof of their guilty. The jury declared them guilty, nonetheless. It took decades for someone to prove their innocence. That was cultural bias.<br /> <br /> Here in Brazil we have clear distinctions between evidence and proof. Specialized analysis isn't an option, but mandatory. A professional assigned by the judge says if a given evidence means what the lawyer says it means. You can doubt this professional, but them you have to bring other specialists to make your case. The judge can't ignore something because she doesn't believe in Carbon-14, or it is against the bible. We are ever more wary of visual witness as evidence.<br /> <br /> People make mistakes. Culture creates a bias that induces some mistakes. And a lay jury isn't prepared to deal with its own bias or mistakes, they aren't eve aware of that. Professionals aren't immune to those things, but at least you have the right to demand it from them.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T09:47:56-07:00","id":638117,"title":"","content":"You example is what I call corruption of a system. Morally, the prosecutor was wrong to do that. A prosecutor is actually charged with finding out the truth, not convicting the accused. The prosecution is not tasked with zeal. In the US, judges don't ignore their emotions, although they are supposed to until sentencing. The human element is the problem, not the jury. When those problems occur, the judge is fully authorized and expected to set aside the verdict of a jury. The US <i>does</i> demand that a jury put aside any bias or emotion. A lay person can do that as well as anybody else. There isn't any real training to be had. You agree to do it and when faced with the crisis, you do it or not. Prosecutors play judges in the US just as easily as juries. You only need to convince 1 out of 12 opposed to 1 judge. Any way you look at it, the accused has an advantage with a jury. A person would have to be universally hated to be disadvantaged by a jury and then you are left with a coin toss with the judge. I'm not saying the system would work as well anywhere else, mind you, I'm saying in the US when we haven't used jury trials the innocent were found guilty by judges much more often. We have a phrase for it, &quot;hanging judge.&quot; If you find yourself on trial in the US and are allowed a jury trial then you better take the jury.<br /> <br /> All that relates to Twilight Judges as don't bring trivial matters, or a weak case, to the judge which seems appropriate, so it works with a US mentality.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1526,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","user":"Diggs","date":". 08-24-2015, 12:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1526-diggs","name":"Diggs","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-08-24T19:40:23-07:00","id":638708,"title":"","content":"Actually, it works with the group mentality in the end, but I agree that it was probably done with the US mentality in mind.<br /> <br /> I won't derail even more the topic explaining it here, but I find the very notion that a lay person can set aside bias and emotion completely ludicrous, no one can do it, and any one who actually think to be able to is just dangerously fooling herself.<br /> <br /> The Twilight Judges work with an European system, probably Romanian system to be precise. But given that they're vampires, probably bias isn't seen as a problem whatsoever. They're academics in a Covenant of academics, anyway, so they probably do a good enough job for the Covenant purposes, as unfair as it can be. And the notion that you have to muster the strength to convince or not need one of them to begin with seems attuned to the Dragons. If it was an Invictus institution, then it would be a different story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}